00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:54.000
They remained on board the vessel near Christmas Island while the Australian government argued over their fate. Their conditions grew more and more bleak. Some of the migrants began a hunger strike. People were sick. People were on the verge of dying. The ship's captain kept trying to get Australia's attention. On the fourth desperate day, he started sailing towards mainland Australia.
在澳大利亚政府就他们的命运争论不休的同时，他们仍然留在圣诞岛附近的船上。他们的处境变得越来越凄凉。一些难民开始绝食抗议。人们生病了。人们处于垂死边缘。船长不断试图引起澳大利亚的注意。在绝望的第四天，他开始向澳大利亚本土驶去。

30
00:17:54.000 --> 00:18:37.000
A high seas standoff with the captain of a Norwegian freighter who picked up more than 400 asylum seekers from a sinking boat and attempted to enter Australian waters. The government refused deploying SAS troops in a show of military force. Besides sending military to prevent the ship from sailing any closer, the Australian government got to work, quickly passing the first of a series of laws, suddenly giving them the power to refuse entry to asylum seekers arriving by boat. And this is an example of what is called a turn back. Australia redirected the asylum seekers to other islands, including a tiny island country called Nauru.
一艘挪威货轮的船长从一艘下沉的船上接走了 400 多名寻求庇护者，并试图进入澳大利亚水域，这引发了一场公海对峙。政府拒绝放行，并部署了特种空勤团（SAS）以展示军事力量。除了派遣军队阻止船只继续航行之外，澳大利亚政府迅速行动，接连通过了一系列法律的第一部分，突然赋予他们拒绝乘船抵达的寻求庇护者入境的权力。这就是所谓的“遣返”（turn back）。澳大利亚将寻求庇护者重新安置到其他岛屿，包括一个名为瑙鲁的微型岛国。

31
00:18:37.000 --> 00:19:22.000
Which is this guano-covered, incredibly hot, isolated island where they detained these asylum seekers. Nauru was once a country rich in natural resources, but after being mined by a series of corrupt governments, it was left desperate for money. And so, when Australia approached them and said, we want to build a detention center on your land, it wasn't really in a position to refuse. Refugees trying to get to Australia would be redirected to Nauru and have their applications for asylum to Australia processed there. And what they decided to do besides deflect their claim is imprison them. Actually, this was the first establishment of a detention center on this, like, offshore island.
这是一个被鸟粪覆盖、炎热无比、极其孤立的岛屿，他们在那里关押这些寻求庇护者。瑙鲁曾是一个自然资源丰富的国家，但在遭到一连串腐败政府的开采后，变得极度缺钱。因此，当澳大利亚找到他们并说“我们想在你们的土地上建立一个拘留中心”时，他们实际上没有立场拒绝。试图前往澳大利亚的难民将被重新安置到瑙鲁，他们在澳大利亚的庇护申请也将在那里处理。他们除了拒绝接受他们的申请之外，还决定将他们监禁。实际上，这是首次在这样的离岸岛屿上建立拘留中心。

32
00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:56.000
And this is a practice that Australia has been doing ever since. What began as a hurried political response to the arrival of that one boat in 2001 grew into a series of permanent immigration policies. Australia called this new set of laws the Pacific Solution. The Pacific Solution involved something else, too. Something kind of extreme. And what they decided to do was actually to excise Christmas Island, as well as a few other islands, from official Australian national territory.
这也是澳大利亚自那以后一直在实行的做法。最初作为对 2001 年那艘船到达的仓促政治回应，最终演变成了一系列永久性的移民政策。澳大利亚将这一套新法律称为“太平洋解决方案”（Pacific Solution）。太平洋解决方案还包含其他内容。某种程度上相当极端的行动。他们决定实际上将圣诞岛以及其他几个岛屿从澳大利亚官方国家领土中剔除。

33
00:19:56.000 --> 00:20:57.000
So that way, if any Afghani or any other person made it to Christmas Island in the future, they couldn't ask for asylum. Just like that, the Australian border was changed. The new policy was backdated to two and a half hours before the refugees entered Australian waters. This way, the official memory of the arrival of those people to Australia had been erased. The asylum seekers had never really been in Australia at all, at least not in any way that could be of benefit to them. The broad point is that the way that people who are trying to keep away migrants talk about borders is that they are these immovable, psychosank lines that are deeply rooted in history.
这样一来，如果未来有任何阿富汗人或其他任何人抵达圣诞岛，他们都无法申请庇护。就这样，澳大利亚的边界被改变了。新政策甚至追溯到难民进入澳大利亚水域前两个半小时生效。通过这种方式，那些人抵达澳大利亚的官方记忆就被抹去了。这些寻求庇护者其实从来没有真正踏上过澳大利亚的土地，至少没有以任何对他们有利的方式踏上过。总的观点是，那些试图将移民拒之门外的人在谈论边境时，总是将其描述为不可动摇、神圣不可侵犯、植根于历史的线条。

34
00:20:57.000 --> 00:21:35.000
But they're not. They are malleable and they are constantly being moved. And that Australia was willing to move its border to protect its border, I think is a really telling example. After the 2001 incident, a detention center was built on Christmas Island too. It became known as the island on which a hundred million crabs roam free, but refugees live behind bars. In the case for open borders, this is John Washington's argument. People should be able to move and migrate wherever they want and need to.
但它们并非如此。它们是可塑的，并且在不断变动。我认为，澳大利亚为了保护其边界而甘愿移动边界，就是一个极具说明意义的例子。2001 年事件发生后，圣诞岛上也建立了一个拘留中心。它被称为“百万只螃蟹自由漫步，但难民却生活在铁窗之后”的岛屿。在《开放边境的理由》中，这就是约翰·华盛顿的论点。人们应该能够随心所欲地四处迁移和移民。

